Here is one implementation of a hash table using a linked list in C++. 

First, let's define the class for our hash table node, which will contain a key-value pair and a pointer to the next node in case of chaining:

```cpp
template <typename K, typename V>
class HashNode {
private:
    K key;
    V value;
    HashNode<K, V> *next;

public:
    HashNode(K key, V value) : key(key), value(value), next(nullptr) {}

    K getKey() const { return key; }
    V getValue() const { return value; }
    void setValue(const V& v) { value = v; }
    HashNode<K, V>* getNext() const { return next; }
    void setNext(HashNode<K, V>* n) { next = n; }
};
```

This class takes two template parameters, K and V, representing the types of the key and value of the hash table. The constructor initializes the key-value pair and sets the next pointer to nullptr. We provide accessor and mutator methods for the key, value, and next pointer.

Next, we define the class for our hash table, which will contain an array of pointers to HashNodes and provide methods for inserting, retrieving, and deleting key-value pairs:

```cpp
template <typename K, typename V>
class HashTable {
private:
    HashNode<K, V> **table;
    int capacity;

    int hashCode(const K& key) const {
        // TODO: implement a hash function for the key
        return 0;
    }

public:
    HashTable(int capacity) : capacity(capacity) {
        table = new HashNode<K, V>*[capacity];
        for (int I = 0; I < capacity; ++i) {
            table[i] = nullptr;
        }
    }

    ~HashTable() {
        for (int I = 0; I < capacity; ++i) {
            HashNode<K, V> *cur = table[i];
            while (cur != nullptr) {
                HashNode<K, V> *next = cur->getNext();
                delete cur;
                cur = next;
            }
            table[i] = nullptr;
        }
        delete[] table;
    }

    void put(const K& key, const V& value) {
        int index = hashCode(key);
        HashNode<K, V> *cur = table[index];
        while (cur != nullptr) {
            if (cur->getKey() == key) {
                cur->setValue(value);
                return;
            }
            cur = cur->getNext();
        }
        cur = new HashNode<K, V>(key, value);
        cur->setNext(table[index]);
        table[index] = cur;
    }

    V get(const K& key) const {
        int index = hashCode(key);
        HashNode<K, V> *cur = table[index];
        while (cur != nullptr) {
            if (cur->getKey() == key) {
                return cur->getValue();
            }
            cur = cur->getNext();
        }
        throw std::out_of_range("key not found");
    }

    void remove(const K& key) {
        int index = hashCode(key);
        HashNode<K, V> *cur = table[index];
        HashNode<K, V> *prev = nullptr;
        while (cur != nullptr) {
            if (cur->getKey() == key) {
                if (prev == nullptr) {
                    table[index] = cur->getNext();
                } else {
                    prev->setNext(cur->getNext());
                }
                delete cur;
                return;
            }
            prev = cur;
            cur = cur->getNext();
        }
        throw std::out_of_range("key not found");
    }
};
```

This class also takes two template parameters, K and V. The constructor initializes the array of HashNode pointers to nullptrs. We provide a destructor that deletes all nodes in the table.

The put method takes a key-value pair and inserts it into the table. If a node with the same key already exists, its value is updated instead. If there are collisions (i.e. The hash code of the key maps to a slot in the array that is already occupied by another node), the new node is chained to the head of the linked list at that slot.

The get method takes a key and returns the corresponding value. If the key is not found in the table, an exception is thrown.

The remove method takes a key and deletes the corresponding node from the table. If the key is not found in the table, an exception is thrown.

We also provide a hashCode method that should be implemented by the user. This method takes a key and returns an integer hash code that maps to a slot in the array. The implementation will depend on the specific use case, but it should ensure a good distribution of keys across the array to minimize collisions.

