Here's a Perl script that reads a directory of images, adds a black border to each image with a thickness of 5 pixels, and saves the modified images with the same filename in a new directory called "bordered_images":

```
#!/usr/bin/perl

use strict;
use warnings;
use Image::Magick;

my $input_dir = "/path/to/input/directory";
my $output_dir = "/path/to/output/directory";

unless (-d $output_dir) {
    mkdir $output_dir or die "Could not create output directory: $!";
}

opendir(my $dh, $input_dir) or die "Could not open input directory: $!";

while (my $file = readdir($dh)) {
    next unless $file =~ /\.(?:jpe?g|png|gif)$/i;

    my $image = Image::Magick->new;
    $image->Read("$input_dir/$file");

    my ($width, $height) = $image->Get('width', 'height');

    $image->Border(
        width => $width + 10,
        height => $height + 10,
        bordercolor => 'black',
        compose => 'Over',
        x => 5,
        y => 5,
    );

    $image->Write("$output_dir/$file");

    print "Processed $file\n";
}

closedir($dh);
```

This script uses the `Image::Magick` Perl module to manipulate the images. It first checks if the output directory exists and creates it if it doesn't. Then it opens the input directory and loops through each file. Only files with extensions of "jpg", "jpeg", "png", or "gif" are processed.

For each file, the script reads the image using `Image::Magick->new` and gets the width and height of the image with `$image->Get('width', 'height')`. It then adds a black border to the image with `$image->Border`. The `x` and `y` parameters are set to 5 to add a 5-pixel border around the image. The modified image is saved to the output directory with `$image->Write`.

Finally, the script prints a message indicating that the file has been processed.

Note that you will need to install the `Image::Magick` Perl module to run this script. You can do this with the following command:

```
sudo cpan Image::Magick
```

