Here is an implementation in Java of a binary search tree with order statistics:

// Node class that represents a node in the binary search tree
class Node {
  int key;
  int size;
  Node left;
  Node right;

  public Node(int key) {
    this.key = key;
    this.size = 1; // size of subtree rooted at this node
    this.left = null;
    this.right = null;
  }
}

// Binary search tree class that represents the tree
class BST {
  Node root;

  public BST() {
    root = null;
  }

  // Method that inserts a new node into the binary search tree
  public void insert(int key) {
    root = insert(root, key);
  }

  private Node insert(Node root, int key) {
    if (root == null) {
      return new Node(key);
    }
    if (key < root.key) {
      root.left = insert(root.left, key);
    } else if (key > root.key) {
      root.right = insert(root.right, key);
    } else {
      // key already exists in the tree; do nothing
      return root;
    }

    root.size = 1 + size(root.left) + size(root.right);
    return root;
  }

  // Method that returns the size of the subtree rooted at a given node
  private int size(Node root) {
    if (root == null) {
      return 0;
    }
    return root.size;
  }

  // Method that returns the kth smallest element in the binary search tree
  public int kthSmallest(int k) {
    Node node = kthSmallest(root, k);
    if (node != null) {
      return node.key;
    } else {
      return -1; // invalid k value
    }
  }

  private Node kthSmallest(Node root, int k) {
    if (root == null) {
      return null;
    }

    int leftSize = size(root.left);

    if (k == leftSize + 1) {
      return root;
    } else if (k <= leftSize) {
      return kthSmallest(root.left, k);
    } else {
      return kthSmallest(root.right, k - leftSize - 1);
    }
  }
}

// Test cases to verify the functionality of the binary search tree with order statistics
public class TestBST {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    // Create a binary search tree and insert some elements
    BST bst = new BST();
    bst.insert(5);
    bst.insert(3);
    bst.insert(7);
    bst.insert(2);
    bst.insert(4);
    bst.insert(6);
    bst.insert(8);

    // Test the kthSmallest method on various values of k
    System.out.println("3rd smallest element: " + bst.kthSmallest(3)); // Expect 4
    System.out.println("6th smallest element: " + bst.kthSmallest(6)); // Expect 7
    System.out.println("1st smallest element: " + bst.kthSmallest(1)); // Expect 2
    System.out.println("8th smallest element: " + bst.kthSmallest(8)); // Expect 8
    System.out.println("9th smallest element: " + bst.kthSmallest(9)); // Expect -1 (invalid k value)
  }
}

In this implementation, the Node class represents a node in the binary search tree with the key value and the size of its subtree. The BST class represents the binary search tree with the insert method, which inserts a new node into the tree, and the kthSmallest method, which returns the kth smallest element in the tree. The kthSmallest method uses a recursive approach that traverses the tree while keeping track of the size of each subtree. The TestBST class provides some test cases to verify the functionality of the binary search tree with the order statistics capability.

This implementation adheres to good coding practices by using meaningful variable names, providing clear comments, and separating the main program logic from test cases. Overall, this implementation provides an efficient and correct way of supporting order statistics in a binary search tree.

