An anagram is a pair of words or phrases that have the same characters arranged in different orders.

Here is an example implementation of a PHP function that checks if two strings are anagrams:

```
function isAnagram($str1, $str2) {
    // Make sure both strings are of the same length
    if (strlen($str1) !== strlen($str2)) {
        return false;
    }

    // Convert both strings to lowercase and remove any whitespace
    $str1 = strtolower(trim($str1));
    $str2 = strtolower(trim($str2));

    // Convert each string to an array of characters
    $arr1 = str_split($str1);
    $arr2 = str_split($str2);

    // Sort both arrays of characters
    sort($arr1);
    sort($arr2);

    // Compare the sorted arrays of characters
    return $arr1 === $arr2;
}
```

We first check that the two input strings are of the same length, since anagrams must have the same number of characters.

Then we convert both strings to lowercase and remove any whitespace, so that the function is case-insensitive and won't be affected by extra spaces in the strings.

Next, we use the `str_split()` function to convert each string to an array of characters, which allows us to sort them using the `sort()` function.

Finally, we simply compare the sorted arrays of characters using the `===` operator, which checks if the two arrays have the same keys and values in the same order. If they do, the function returns `true` indicating that the two strings are anagrams. Otherwise, it returns `false`.

